WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:00.390
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:00.390 --> 00:00:02.640
SARA ROBINSON: What if Google
Cloud Platform's machine

00:00:02.640 --> 00:00:04.710
learning tools were all
part of one pyramid?

00:00:10.460 --> 00:00:13.700
Literally, as I was trying to
fall asleep, I had an idea.

00:00:13.700 --> 00:00:16.070
I'm Sara Robinson, and I'd
like to help you figure out

00:00:16.070 --> 00:00:19.640
which ML Tool is right for the
problem you're trying to solve.

00:00:19.640 --> 00:00:21.620
Google has a bunch of
different ML products

00:00:21.620 --> 00:00:23.900
to get you from
point A to point B--

00:00:23.900 --> 00:00:26.540
in order to provide flexibility
in choosing which path

00:00:26.540 --> 00:00:29.150
you want to take, based
on your cost effort

00:00:29.150 --> 00:00:30.830
and speed requirements.

00:00:30.830 --> 00:00:32.840
So I wrote it all
out really quickly.

00:00:32.840 --> 00:00:35.630
Then, I edited it the
next day and sent it out.

00:00:35.630 --> 00:00:37.160
I guess it resonated.

00:00:37.160 --> 00:00:39.830
The idea is, as you
move up the pyramid,

00:00:39.830 --> 00:00:41.853
less ML expertise is required.

00:00:41.853 --> 00:00:43.520
And you also don't
need to worry as much

00:00:43.520 --> 00:00:45.470
about the infrastructure
behind your model.

00:00:45.470 --> 00:00:48.110
The pyramid is organized
by levels of abstraction.

00:00:48.110 --> 00:00:49.700
Let's start at the bottom.

00:00:49.700 --> 00:00:51.630
At the lowest level
of abstraction,

00:00:51.630 --> 00:00:53.180
we have these
popular open source

00:00:53.180 --> 00:00:54.920
machine learning frameworks.

00:00:54.920 --> 00:00:57.800
If you're building a model from
scratch using these frameworks,

00:00:57.800 --> 00:00:59.930
you get the flexibility
of moving your workloads

00:00:59.930 --> 00:01:02.960
across different development
and deployment environments.

00:01:02.960 --> 00:01:05.600
As a result, you need to
manage all the infrastructure

00:01:05.600 --> 00:01:07.860
yourself, for
training and serving.

00:01:07.860 --> 00:01:09.620
If your team has
very specific needs,

00:01:09.620 --> 00:01:12.380
and you have the ML engineers
and data scientists,

00:01:12.380 --> 00:01:14.120
you might prefer the
total flexibility

00:01:14.120 --> 00:01:16.940
of using these open source
ML frameworks on their own

00:01:16.940 --> 00:01:19.550
and figuring out where
to train and serve them.

00:01:19.550 --> 00:01:22.280
Moving up one tier, Deep
Learning Virtual Machines

00:01:22.280 --> 00:01:24.590
provide managed
click-to-deploy VMs

00:01:24.590 --> 00:01:27.230
for processing data and
training your model.

00:01:27.230 --> 00:01:29.510
These VMs come with
popular ML frameworks

00:01:29.510 --> 00:01:32.840
pre-installed, but leave you to
figure out exactly how you'll

00:01:32.840 --> 00:01:33.980
serve those models.

00:01:33.980 --> 00:01:36.200
For instance, you could
serve them on-premise

00:01:36.200 --> 00:01:38.720
or directly on a mobile device.

00:01:38.720 --> 00:01:41.270
With Deep Learning VMs,
you can reduce the overhead

00:01:41.270 --> 00:01:44.120
of managing and allocating the
compute and storage resources

00:01:44.120 --> 00:01:46.000
required for ML workloads.

00:01:46.000 --> 00:01:47.930
Kubeflow is an
open source project

00:01:47.930 --> 00:01:50.090
created by Google
for deploying your ML

00:01:50.090 --> 00:01:51.740
workloads on Kubernetes.

00:01:51.740 --> 00:01:54.890
You use it to configure
a multi-step ML pipeline,

00:01:54.890 --> 00:01:57.440
with different components
for pre-processing data,

00:01:57.440 --> 00:01:59.540
training, and
serving your model.

00:01:59.540 --> 00:02:01.340
Because it's open
source, you can run it

00:02:01.340 --> 00:02:03.590
on-premise or on AnyCloud.

00:02:03.590 --> 00:02:06.350
The other way of saying that
is, what you'll still need to do

00:02:06.350 --> 00:02:08.419
is configure where it's managed.

00:02:08.419 --> 00:02:11.030
With Kubeflow, you can leverage
the power of containers

00:02:11.030 --> 00:02:13.340
to make your ML stack
portable, reducing

00:02:13.340 --> 00:02:16.600
the operational overhead
of running your workloads.

00:02:16.600 --> 00:02:19.560
AI Platform takes this "where"
question out of the equation.

00:02:19.560 --> 00:02:22.340
It's all handled on Google
Cloud Platform, our managed

00:02:22.340 --> 00:02:25.020
service for all your
custom model needs.

00:02:25.020 --> 00:02:27.177
AI Platform is
entirely server-less,

00:02:27.177 --> 00:02:29.510
so you can focus on your
specific workloads and business

00:02:29.510 --> 00:02:30.060
logic.

00:02:30.060 --> 00:02:32.227
The platform includes tools
for training and serving

00:02:32.227 --> 00:02:35.120
models, post to Notebooks,
a data-labeling service,

00:02:35.120 --> 00:02:36.330
and more.

00:02:36.330 --> 00:02:38.330
For example, you could
take the same Notebook

00:02:38.330 --> 00:02:40.490
code you're running
on-premise with Kubeflow,

00:02:40.490 --> 00:02:43.610
and run it on GCP with
AI Platform Notebooks.

00:02:43.610 --> 00:02:45.170
And something else
I should mention,

00:02:45.170 --> 00:02:47.630
you don't have to use AI
Platform for both training

00:02:47.630 --> 00:02:48.750
and serving.

00:02:48.750 --> 00:02:52.070
If you wanted, you could let AI
Platform handle one, and figure

00:02:52.070 --> 00:02:53.460
out the other on your own.

00:02:53.460 --> 00:02:56.750
For example, you could take
advantage of AI Platform's TPUs

00:02:56.750 --> 00:03:00.020
to train your model, and
then serve it on-device.

00:03:00.020 --> 00:03:02.370
By the time we get to
BigQuery Machine Learning,

00:03:02.370 --> 00:03:05.600
you no longer need a team of ML
engineers and data scientists.

00:03:05.600 --> 00:03:07.850
But it might still be
helpful to have a few.

00:03:07.850 --> 00:03:10.280
Unlike the products
below it, with BQML,

00:03:10.280 --> 00:03:12.675
you don't have to write any
of the underlying model code.

00:03:12.675 --> 00:03:14.300
You'll still need to
choose which model

00:03:14.300 --> 00:03:17.000
type you want to use, when you
run your create model query.

00:03:17.000 --> 00:03:19.680
Running this query will
create and train your model.

00:03:19.680 --> 00:03:22.310
And once you run it, your model
will be automatically deployed

00:03:22.310 --> 00:03:24.260
for you, and available
to make predictions

00:03:24.260 --> 00:03:26.780
with a single SQL query.

00:03:26.780 --> 00:03:29.270
With BQML, you can
bring the power of ML

00:03:29.270 --> 00:03:31.250
closer to where the
data is analyzed

00:03:31.250 --> 00:03:33.232
and make it accessible
to data analysts.

00:03:33.232 --> 00:03:35.190
But what if you didn't
need to write model code

00:03:35.190 --> 00:03:37.220
or choose your model type?

00:03:37.220 --> 00:03:39.140
That's where AutoML comes in.

00:03:39.140 --> 00:03:41.700
All you'll need to do is
gather and upload your data,

00:03:41.700 --> 00:03:45.650
whether it's images, video,
text, or structured data.

00:03:45.650 --> 00:03:48.950
Then, once you press the Train
button and training completes,

00:03:48.950 --> 00:03:51.230
your model is immediately
available for prediction

00:03:51.230 --> 00:03:52.610
via an API.

00:03:52.610 --> 00:03:54.900
You don't need to
deploy it yourself.

00:03:54.900 --> 00:03:58.250
You can also use AutoML's UI
to evaluate your model quality

00:03:58.250 --> 00:03:59.570
and update training data.

00:03:59.570 --> 00:04:01.790
AutoML democratizes
machine learning

00:04:01.790 --> 00:04:03.830
by empowering more
people to build custom

00:04:03.830 --> 00:04:06.830
ML models that solve a wide
variety of business problems,

00:04:06.830 --> 00:04:09.620
regardless of ML expertise.

00:04:09.620 --> 00:04:13.010
For both AutoML and the
tip of the pyramid, APIs,

00:04:13.010 --> 00:04:15.440
you don't need any ML
engineers or data scientists

00:04:15.440 --> 00:04:16.279
on your team--

00:04:16.279 --> 00:04:18.110
just a handful of developers.

00:04:18.110 --> 00:04:20.060
What makes our APIs
different from AutoML,

00:04:20.060 --> 00:04:22.610
though, is that you don't need
to supply any training data

00:04:22.610 --> 00:04:23.630
yourself.

00:04:23.630 --> 00:04:25.220
Just make an API
Request, and you'll

00:04:25.220 --> 00:04:29.150
get access to pre-trained models
for images, video, speech,

00:04:29.150 --> 00:04:30.910
text, and translation.

00:04:30.910 --> 00:04:34.040
Our ML APIs are the easiest
and fastest way to get

00:04:34.040 --> 00:04:35.960
started with AI today.

00:04:35.960 --> 00:04:36.920
I'm Sara Robinson.

00:04:36.920 --> 00:04:38.633
And hopefully you're
not losing any sleep

00:04:38.633 --> 00:04:40.550
over choosing the machine
learning tool that's

00:04:40.550 --> 00:04:41.870
going to help you the most.

00:04:41.870 --> 00:04:43.710
Leave that part to me.

00:04:43.710 --> 00:04:45.950
Be sure to check out the
GCP YouTube channel for more

00:04:45.950 --> 00:04:47.505
relevant informative content.

00:04:47.505 --> 00:04:49.130
And if you found this
helpful, don't be

00:04:49.130 --> 00:04:50.700
afraid to like and subscribe.

00:04:50.700 --> 00:04:51.830
Thanks for watching.

00:04:51.830 --> 00:04:55.180
[THEME MUSIC]

